The files below are derived from that work. Every one of them has been translated from
Kotlin to Swift and adapted to SwiftUI's layout and animation model, so none is a
verbatim copy; where behaviour was deliberately left out or changed, the file's own
documentation says so.

Derived from androidx.compose.material3.carousel:

    Sources/ExpressiveUI/Components/Carousel/CarouselArrangement.swift
    Sources/ExpressiveUI/Components/Carousel/CarouselKeyline.swift
    Sources/ExpressiveUI/Components/Carousel/CarouselKeylines.swift
    Sources/ExpressiveUI/Components/Carousel/CarouselStrategy.swift
    Sources/ExpressiveUI/Components/Carousel/ExpressiveCarousel.swift (per-item layout)

Derived from androidx.graphics.shapes, and from the figures MaterialShapes publishes:

    Sources/ExpressiveUI/Components/Progress/ExpressiveRoundedPolygon.swift
    Sources/ExpressiveUI/Components/Progress/ExpressiveMaterialShape.swift

Derived from CubicBezierEasing in androidx.compose.animation.core:

    Sources/ExpressiveUI/Components/Progress/ExpressiveCubicBezier.swift

Elsewhere in the library the measurements, durations, easing curves and colour roles are
taken from the published Material 3 design tokens. Those are used as the specification
states them rather than copied as code.

